1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
One sky. Han China said: the earth
always moves — unnoticed.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,500
A Han-dynasty text, the Kaolingyao,
makes a bold claim: the earth wanders.

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:16,000
Winter solstice: the earth shifts 30,000
li up, north and west. Summer: back the
other way.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
At the equinoxes it sits in the middle.
Once a year, there and back.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,500
So why don’t we feel it? The text goes
on:

6
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:34,500
Picture yourself in a big boat’s cabin,
with the windows shut.

7
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,000
The boat moves. You feel nothing.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
From the shore, the boat moves. From the
cabin, all is still.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:55,500
About 1,600 years later, in 1632,
Galileo put people in a ship’s cabin too
— to show we can’t feel Earth move.

10
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:02,500
But it’s not the same motion: a yearly
to-and-fro, versus Earth spinning and
circling the Sun.

11
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:11,500
What’s shared is the insight: from
inside, you can’t feel your own motion.
So don’t say China found it first.

12
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Mandar: the same Dipper becomes a
fishing net.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

